{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ויבן שם מזבח", :translit "va'yiven sham mizbe'ach", :gloss "and he built there an altar"} {:he "ויהי בימי אמרפל מלך שנער וגו'", :translit "va'yehi bi'mei amrafel melekh shin'ar ve'gomer", :gloss "and it came to pass in the days of Amraphel king of Shinar etc."} {:he "עשו מלחמה", :translit "asu milchamah", :gloss "they made war"} {:he "יסכור כל מנחותיך ועולתך ידשנה סלה", :translit "yizkor kol minchotecha ve'olatecha yidsheneh selah", :gloss "may He remember all your meal-offerings and your burnt-offering He will accept selah"} {:he "יתן לך כלבבך וכל עצתך ימלא", :translit "yiten lekha kilvavekha ve'chol atatecha yemalei", :gloss "may He give you your heart's desire and all your counsel fulfill"} {:he "נרננה בישועתך ובשם אלהינו נדגול", :translit "narna'nah bi'yeshu'atecha u'vi'shem elohenu nidgol", :gloss "we will sing for joy in your salvation and in the name of our God we will set up banners"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhah: the verse after the altar-building narrates war, taken as a hint that one brings an offering before battle."}], :frame {:text "Before going out to war, the Torah requires preparing a sacrificial offering. The Talmud derives that the priest appointed over warfare speaks the verses from Psalms 20:4–6, which mention offerings followed by confidence in battle. Ba'al HaTurim reads the juxtaposition of Abraham's altar-building (Genesis 13:18) with the war narrative (Genesis 14:2) as encoding this practice in the verse order itself.", :sources ["Sotah 42a" "Psalms 20:4–6" "Genesis 14:2"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhah: the verse after the altar-building narrates war, taken as a hint that one brings an offering before battle.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:05:48.187063357Z", :model "glm-5.2"}